+fnptr_nested_permutation	void (*(*p)(int))(char);	CC_TYPEOF_P	void (*(*)(int))(char)	0	1	The named-declarator path folds nested function-pointer declarators in the wrong order: `void (*(*p)(int))(char)` builds parameters `(char)` and one pointer level too many in the innermost return type, while the abstract declarator path builds the right type. The predicate is then comparing two different types and correctly answers 0.	Calls through such pointers at -O1 match GCC, and lccc/GCC mixed-object linking works, so today's ABI impact is nil — but the predicate is a *witness*: the whole tree's worth of `__same_type`/`__builtin_types_compatible_p` assertions over nested function pointers is unguarded until the folding is fixed.	src/frontend/parser/types.rs — derived-declarator folding for a NAMED declarator whose parenthesised core is a function pointer (`(*(*p)(int))(...)`), i.e. `combine_declarator_parts`/`build_full_ctype_with_base`, not the predicate.
